WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.314
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:14.978 --> 00:00:21.983
Welcome back to Superstars in History, where I chat with famous figures from the past!

3
00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:29.528
Today's guest was an American president who led his nation through the tough times of the Civil War.

4
00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:36.920
You might know him as the president who abolished slavery in the U.S., but the truth is a

5
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.921
bit more complicated than that.

6
00:00:39.921 --> 00:00:42.583
Please welcome Abraham Lincoln!

7
00:00:57.611 --> 00:00:59.332
Thank you for having me, Faye.

8
00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:02.494
Thanks for joining us, President Lincoln!

9
00:01:03.475 --> 00:01:07.479
I can't wait to hear how you became one of the most famous U.S.

10
00:01:07.559 --> 00:01:08.600
presidents ever.

11
00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.843
Why don't we start with your childhood?

12
00:01:13.024 --> 00:01:16.087
I was born in 1809 in Hodgenville, Kentucky.

13
00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:21.011
When I was seven, my father lost almost all of his land in lawsuits.

14
00:01:21.952 --> 00:01:26.016
We moved to Indiana, where there were more opportunities to buy new land.

15
00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:30.775
We arrived with very little and lived in a log cabin my father built.

16
00:01:31.736 --> 00:01:34.819
It sounds like your father was pretty handy!

17
00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:36.740
What was your mother like?

18
00:01:37.961 --> 00:01:41.904
Sadly my mother died when I was young, but my father remarried.

19
00:01:42.985 --> 00:01:44.767
I was very close with my stepmother.

20
00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:48.370
She encouraged me to read and learn as much as I could.

21
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.894
I didn't have the time or money to go to school, so I taught myself almost everything.

22
00:01:55.035 --> 00:01:58.177
I loved to learn, both as a child and throughout my life.

23
00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:01.019
That's impressive!

24
00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:04.761
But eventually you left Indiana, right?

25
00:02:05.581 --> 00:02:06.142
That's true!

26
00:02:07.102 --> 00:02:09.844
When I was 21, my family moved to Illinois.

27
00:02:10.764 --> 00:02:13.346
I did a few odd jobs but didn't make much money.

28
00:02:14.206 --> 00:02:18.308
Eventually an acquaintance named John Todd Stuart encouraged me to become a lawyer.

29
00:02:19.229 --> 00:02:20.910
I studied the law and taught myself.

30
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.692
Stuart hired me at his firm once I got my law license.

31
00:02:25.632 --> 00:02:31.097
People liked the way I was straightforward and fair in court, so they started calling me "Honest Abe."

32
00:02:32.197 --> 00:02:35.520
Wow, you were self-taught in law too!

33
00:02:36.401 --> 00:02:39.704
You also started a family of your own around this time.

34
00:02:41.025 --> 00:02:46.029
Yes. I married my wife, Mary Todd, a cousin of John's, in 1842.

35
00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:51.533
Together we had four sons, but two died when they were only children.

36
00:02:52.594 --> 00:02:54.696
We were very sad when we lost our boys.

37
00:02:55.752 --> 00:02:57.453
I'm sorry for your loss.

38
00:02:58.473 --> 00:02:59.293
Let's move on.

39
00:03:00.233 --> 00:03:01.994
How did you get into politics?

40
00:03:03.114 --> 00:03:08.715
While training to be a lawyer, I decided to run for the Illinois state legislature and was elected

41
00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:09.676
for several years.

42
00:03:10.776 --> 00:03:14.777
Eventually I spoke out against the Mexican-American War that was being fought at the time.

43
00:03:15.697 --> 00:03:20.319
This upset many voters, so I decided not to run for reelection after that.

44
00:03:21.619 --> 00:03:25.420
The Mexican-American War wasn't the only big issue at the time.

45
00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:30.215
Slavery was also being hotly debated.

46
00:03:31.276 --> 00:03:34.739
Abolitionists in the North were calling for a complete end to slavery.

47
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.783
This concerned the South because its economy depended on slaves who worked the farms.

48
00:03:40.764 --> 00:03:46.509
The South was also worried about the federal government growing too powerful; many thought states should have more

49
00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:47.810
power and independence.

50
00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.172
Meanwhile the U.S.

51
00:03:50.212 --> 00:03:55.276
was still expanding westward, and the new states had to decide if they would allow slavery or not.

52
00:03:56.396 --> 00:03:58.237
What was your position on the issue?

53
00:03:59.237 --> 00:04:05.879
I had always hated slavery, but I didn't support abolition, since it didn't seem possible under the Constitution.

54
00:04:06.899 --> 00:04:11.420
In states where slavery already existed, I thought the states should be able to choose whether to keep

55
00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.180
it or end it.

56
00:04:13.261 --> 00:04:15.701
I didn't want slavery to expand into new states though.

57
00:04:16.441 --> 00:04:20.983
I started speaking out more vehemently on the issue and debated it with my opponent when I ran

58
00:04:21.023 --> 00:04:21.503
for the Senate.

59
00:04:22.383 --> 00:04:27.501
Although I lost the election, our debates were very popular, and I gained attention from voters.

60
00:04:28.582 --> 00:04:31.003
Is that when you decided to run for president?

61
00:04:32.263 --> 00:04:34.725
Many people encouraged me to run, so I did.

62
00:04:35.705 --> 00:04:38.347
And in 1861, I became president!

63
00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.508
I'm afraid my victory angered the South, though.

64
00:04:42.189 --> 00:04:48.152
Seven Southern states joined together and declared themselves their own nation, the Confederate States of America.

65
00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:55.476
I believed leaving the United States—leaving the Union—was an illegal action, but I didn't want war.

66
00:04:56.448 --> 00:04:58.610
That's why I refused to make the first strike.

67
00:04:59.491 --> 00:05:04.976
But when a commander asked for supplies in Fort Sumter, South Carolina, I sent what he asked for.

68
00:05:05.917 --> 00:05:10.721
The South took that as an action against them, and their army started firing on Fort Sumter.

69
00:05:11.582 --> 00:05:13.343
The Civil War had begun.

70
00:05:14.204 --> 00:05:17.907
You were president for only a few months when war broke out!

71
00:05:18.748 --> 00:05:20.369
That must have been tough.

72
00:05:21.791 --> 00:05:22.812
Indeed it was.

73
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.722
I took control and shaped our military strategy.

74
00:05:27.442 --> 00:05:31.965
There was a string of commanders for my Union army, but none was able to carry out a

75
00:05:32.045 --> 00:05:34.847
large-scale attack on the Confederacy in the South.

76
00:05:35.887 --> 00:05:37.408
I finally found Ulysses S.

77
00:05:37.448 --> 00:05:39.670
Grant, whom I named general in chief.

78
00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.032
He was able to command the army the way I wanted.

79
00:05:43.912 --> 00:05:47.974
Meanwhile I was trying to figure out the future of slavery in the country.

80
00:05:48.895 --> 00:05:50.336
We know what you decided.

81
00:05:51.524 --> 00:05:58.410
In 1863 you issued the Emancipation Proclamation, which freed slaves in the Confederacy.

82
00:05:59.331 --> 00:06:05.556
You also gave one of the most famous speeches in history: the Gettysburg Address.

83
00:06:07.337 --> 00:06:07.878
Ah, yes.

84
00:06:08.238 --> 00:06:12.782
In my Gettysburg Address, I made it clear the war was about liberty and freedom for all.

85
00:06:14.003 --> 00:06:15.544
I'm told people loved the speech.

86
00:06:16.525 --> 00:06:18.887
Perhaps it even contributed to my reelection in 1864.

87
00:06:20.772 --> 00:06:24.775
Unfortunately, Faye, the war was still going on when I started my second term.

88
00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:31.800
Congress resolved the slavery issue in 1865 when they drafted and passed the Thirteenth Amendment to the U.S.

89
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.762
Constitution, abolishing slavery.

90
00:06:34.782 --> 00:06:39.446
I hadn't supported full abolition in the past, but I did support this amendment.

91
00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:47.772
Later that year Confederate General Lee surrendered to General Grant in Appomattox, Virginia, officially ending the war.

92
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.123
You must have been relieved that the country could be one union again.

93
00:06:54.064 --> 00:06:55.905
Yes, but it wasn't so simple.

94
00:06:56.825 --> 00:06:59.527
I knew I needed to work hard to rebuild the country.

95
00:07:00.408 --> 00:07:06.092
Many Americans wanted to punish the South, but I was forgiving since it was important to keep the

96
00:07:06.132 --> 00:07:06.892
country together.

97
00:07:07.813 --> 00:07:14.537
There were people with strong feelings on both sides, and that wasn't going away any time soon, if

98
00:07:14.657 --> 00:07:18.260
ever. I think you're right about that, President Lincoln.

99
00:07:19.256 --> 00:07:21.118
Thank you so much for joining us.

100
00:07:22.079 --> 00:07:24.241
We'll let you get back to history now.

101
00:07:25.282 --> 00:07:36.252
Good-bye! Five days after General Lee surrendered, Abraham Lincoln attended a play in Washington DC with his wife.

102
00:07:37.333 --> 00:07:44.619
He sat in a private box in Ford's Theater, where he was shot by Confederate sympathizer John Wilkes

103
00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:52.746
Booth. President Abraham Lincoln died the next morning on April 15, 1865.

104
00:07:53.887 --> 00:07:59.773
Abraham Lincoln is remembered for the roles he played in abolishing slavery and preserving the U.S.

105
00:07:59.813 --> 00:08:02.235
as a democracy and one nation.

106
00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:08.521
Many see "Honest Abe" as a hero—no wonder his face is on the U.S.

107
00:08:08.601 --> 00:08:11.524
penny! Thanks for tuning in today.

108
00:08:12.456 --> 00:08:17.042
I'll be back again soon with another episode of Superstars in History!
